  ### Babylon rendering section

  **`buildBeadMesh`** (lines 841–947) builds the actual extruded "tube" mesh per line-type (elliptical cross-section along the tangent, oriented by nozzle axis). Signature:
  ```ts
  function buildBeadMesh(
    name: string, parsed: ParsedGCode, sampler: PathSampler,
    include: (k: number) => boolean, material: BABYLON.Material, scene: BABYLON.Scene,
  ): PathMesh
  ```
  It takes **one material for the entire mesh** (`mesh.material = material;` line 944) — i.e. coloring today is **per-mesh material, not per-vertex color**. There is no `VertexData.colors` array anywhere in this function (only `positions`/`normals`/`indices`, lines 895–897, 939–943).

  **`buildPathMesh`** (lines 951–1002) — for non-extrusion strokes (travel/wipe), builds a `BABYLON.LinesMesh`:
  ```ts
  function buildPathMesh(
    name: string, parsed: ParsedGCode, sampler: PathSampler,
    include: (k: number) => boolean, color: BABYLON.Color3, scene: BABYLON.Scene,
  ): PathMesh
  ```
  Colored via `mesh.color = color;` (line 998) — again **one flat color per whole LinesMesh**, no per-vertex/per-segment color buffer.

  **`buildMarkerMesh`** (lines 1005–1030) — thin-instanced spheres, one shared `StandardMaterial` per call with `material.emissiveColor = color` (line 1016); all instances of a marker type share that single color.

  **Material/color construction loop** (lines 1226–1239):
  ```ts
  const colors = Object.fromEntries(
    LINE_TYPES.map((t) => [t.id, BABYLON.Color3.FromHexString(t.color)]),
  ) as Record<LineTypeId, BABYLON.Color3>;
  const beadMaterials = new Map<LineTypeId, BABYLON.StandardMaterial>();
  for (const t of LINE_TYPES) {
    if (t.kind !== 'extrusion') continue;
    const mat = new BABYLON.StandardMaterial(`${GCODE_MESH_PREFIX}BeadMat_${t.id}`, scene);
    mat.diffuseColor = colors[t.id];
    mat.emissiveColor = colors[t.id].scale(0.18);
    mat.specularColor = new BABYLON.Color3(0.25, 0.25, 0.25);
    mat.specularPower = 48;
    mat.backFaceCulling = false;
    beadMaterials.set(t.id, mat);
  }
  ```
  This builds exactly one discrete `StandardMaterial` per **extrusion** `LineTypeId` (11 of the 16 line types are `kind: 'extrusion'`; the rest — travel/retraction/unretraction/wipe/seam — reuse the flat `colors[type]` `Color3` directly rather than a material). `LINE_TYPES` order/colors are read once at layer-creation time (module scope of `createGCodeLayer`), not per-load.

  ### Mesh building & per-type grouping at `load()` (lines 1406–1447)

  ```ts
  load(next) {
    disposeAll();
    parsed = next;
    ...
    for (const t of LINE_TYPES) {
      if (t.kind !== 'extrusion') continue;
      const include = (k: number) => moves[k].type === t.id;
      const built = buildBeadMesh(`${GCODE_MESH_PREFIX}Bead_${t.id}`, next, pathSampler, include, beadMaterials.get(t.id)!, scene);
      paths.push({ ...built, type: t.id, include });
    }
    const travelInclude = (k: number) => !moves[k].extruding && moves[k].type !== 'wipe';
    const wipeInclude = (k: number) => moves[k].type === 'wipe';
    for (const [type, include] of [['travel', travelInclude], ['wipe', wipeInclude]] as const) {
      const built = buildPathMesh(`${GCODE_MESH_PREFIX}Path_${type}`, next, pathSampler, include, colors[type], scene);
      paths.push({ ...built, type, include });
    }
    for (const [type, at] of Object.entries(next.markers) as [LineTypeId, Uint32Array][]) {
      const mesh = buildMarkerMesh(`${GCODE_MESH_PREFIX}Marker_${type}`, at, pathSampler, colors[type], scene);
      if (mesh) markers.push({ type, mesh, at });
    }
    ...
  }
  ```

  So today: **one mesh per discrete `LineTypeId`**, each with a fixed material/color, and `include(k)` is a simple predicate `moves[k].type === t.id`. Visibility toggling (`setLineTypeVisibility`, lines 1453–1456) just does `entry.mesh?.setEnabled(visibility[entry.type])` per mesh (`applyVisibility`, lines 1390–1394) — it never rebuilds geometry, only flips `mesh.isEnabled`.

  **What would need to change for a continuous numeric gradient color-by mode:**
  1. `GCodeMove` (or a parallel array like `feeds[]`) would need to carry the numeric field per move (speed, temp, fan, layer height, width) — currently discarded after parsing (feeds/eDeltas/deposits/accels are local to `parseGCode`, not returned).
  2. `buildBeadMesh`/`buildPathMesh` build one flat-colored mesh per category via a single `material`/`color` parameter; to gradient-color a continuous value, per-vertex colors would need to be added to the `VertexData` (`data.colors = ...`), and the material would need `StandardMaterial.useVertexColors`-style handling (Babylon: setting `mesh.material` on a mesh with vertex colors requires `material.disableLighting`/`emissiveColor` tricks, or a per-vertex-color-aware material) — none of that plumbing exists today.
  3. Since meshes today are grouped **one per discrete `LineTypeId`** (`include(k) = moves[k].type === t.id`), a numeric color-by would likely need either (a) a single big mesh with per-vertex color driven by the numeric value (min/max normalized), replacing the per-type split entirely for that mode, or (b) many meshes bucketed by value ranges — a bigger structural change, not a drop-in swap of the material.
  4. No gradient/lerp helper exists in this file or anywhere in `src/components` (`BABYLON.Color3.Lerp` unused repo-wide) — `writeRing`'s "gradient" comment (line 807) refers to a normal-vector gradient of an ellipse equation, unrelated to color interpolation. Any numeric colormap would be built from scratch.
  5. The live/in-progress segment (`liveBead`/`liveLine`, `updateLive()`, lines 1346–1388) also picks its color from `beadMaterials.get(m.type)` / `colors[type]` (lines 1374, 1381) — would need equivalent gradient logic for the "live" trailing segment too.

